.TH SUNOS 4 "2026-10-08" "Substrate" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
sunos \- SunOS 4 (Sun386i) execution personality
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B SunOS
personality
.RB ( PERS_SUNOS )
runs the programs of SunOS 4.0 for the Sun386i, Sun's 80386 workstation.
It is rooted at
.IR /perso/sunos ,
which is where a program's absolute paths are looked for first and where
the files it makes are made.
.PP
SunOS 4 is 4.3BSD with Sun's additions, and this is a BSD personality:
BSD's system calls and their numbers, BSD's signals and
.BR sigvec (2),
BSD's
.B struct stat
and error numbers.  It shares one thing with the System V personalities
.RB ( svr3 (4),
.BR svr4 (4)),
the file format its programs are stored in, and nothing else.
.SS Executable format
Where every other Sun's programs are
.BR a.out ,
the Sun386i's are i386 COFF:
.B f_magic
0x14c, an optional header with magic 0413.  The COFF loader tells one from
a System V Release 3 program by where its text is.  Sun's linker leaves
the first page of the address space empty and puts the file's first page,
headers and all, at 0x1000, so the text starts at 0x10d0; a Release 3
program's starts in the first page.  A program whose text starts in the
second page is given this personality and is never run as a System V one.
.PP
The file is mapped as it lies:
.B .text
from 0x1000,
.B .data
on the page boundary after it,
.B .bss
after that, where the break starts.  Address 0 is not mapped, and a
program that uses a null pointer is sent
.BR SIGSEGV .
The stack is at the top of user space and starts with
.IR argc ,
.IR argv[] ,
a null pointer,
.IR envp[] ,
and a null pointer.
.SS Shared libraries
Nearly every program is dynamically linked, and the kernel has no part in
it.  The kernel loads the program alone.  The program's own startup code
opens
.I /lib/ld.so
and maps it with
.BR mmap (2);
.B ld.so
finds
.I /usr/lib/libc.so.2.0
by reading the directory, and maps that.  Memory that is to start as
zeroes is a mapping of
.IR /dev/zero .
A program linked with
.B "cc \-Bstatic"
does none of this and runs the same.
.SS System call interface
A system call is
.BR "int $0xff" :
the call's number in
.BR EAX ,
the arguments on the stack as for a C function, above the return address
of the library stub that made the call.  The result is in
.BR EAX .
On failure the carry flag is set and
.B EAX
holds the error number.  Some calls give a second result in
.BR EDX :
.BR getpid (2)
the parent's process ID,
.BR getuid (2)
and
.BR getgid (2)
the effective IDs,
.BR pipe (2)
the second descriptor, and
.BR fork (2)
and
.BR vfork (2)
1 in the child and 0 in the parent.
.PP
Vector 0xff is the kernel's own, so the instruction faults, and the
personality's trap hook recognises it and dispatches the call.
.PP
The numbers are those of SunOS 4.0's
.IR <sys/syscall.h> .
What differs from substrate's own calls is translated:
.TP
.BR open (2), " fcntl" (2)
the flag bits
.RB ( FNDELAY ,
.BR FAPPEND ,
.BR FCREAT ,
.BR FTRUNC ,
.BR FEXCL ,
.BR FNBIO ,
.BR FSYNC ).
.TP
.BR dup (2)
with 0100 set in the descriptor is
.BR dup2 (2),
which is how the Bourne shell calls it.
.TP
.BR stat (2), " lstat" (2), " fstat" (2)
4.3BSD's 64-byte
.BR "struct stat" .
.TP
.BR getdents (2)
.B struct dirent
with
.BR d_off ,
.BR d_fileno ,
.B d_reclen
and
.BR d_namlen .
.TP
.BR statfs (2), " fstatfs" (2)
SunOS's
.BR "struct statfs" .
A file that is not a directory is answered for by its directory.
.TP
.BR mmap (2)
returns the address when the caller sets
.B _MAP_NEW
(as libc's
.B mmap()
does) and 0 otherwise.  A mapping whose address is left to the system is
placed from 0x40000000 up, clear of the program and its heap.
.TP
.BR brk (2)
returns 0.
.TP
.BR wait4 (2)
a process ID of 0 means any child.
.TP
.BR getrlimit (2), " setrlimit" (2)
the six resources of 4.3BSD;
.B RLIM_INFINITY
is 0x7fffffff.
.TP
.BR ioctl (2)
the terminal requests of
.I <sys/termios.h>
and
.I <sys/termio.h>
.RB ( TCGETS ,
.BR TCSETS ,
.BR TCGETA
and the rest), those of the old terminal driver
.RB ( TIOCGETP ,
.BR TIOCSETP ,
.BR TIOCGETC ,
.BR TIOCGLTC ,
.B TIOCLGET
and the rest), the window size and process group of a terminal, and
.BR FIONREAD ,
.BR FIONBIO ,
.BR FIOCLEX
and
.BR FIONCLEX .
A terminal's speed is reported as 9600 baud.
.SS Signals
The signals are numbered as 4.3BSD numbered them.
.BR sigvec (2),
.BR sigblock (2),
.BR sigsetmask (2),
.BR sigpause (2)
and
.BR sigstack (2)
are provided.  A system call interrupted by a caught signal is restarted
unless the handler was installed with
.BR SV_INTERRUPT .
.PP
A handler is entered with the signal's number, a code, the address of a
.B struct sigcontext
and a fault address on the stack, and no return address.  The handler the
kernel is given is libc's
.BR _sigtramp ,
which calls the program's and returns through the
.B sigcleanup
call (139) with the stack pointer at the address of the
.BR sigcontext .
The context holds what
.I <sys/signal.h>
defines for the Sun386i:
.BR sc_onstack ,
.BR sc_mask ,
.BR sc_sp ,
.BR sc_pc ,
.BR sc_ps ,
.B sc_eax
and
.BR sc_edx .
.SS Files and devices
The personality works in its own tree.  An absolute path names the file
under
.I /perso/sunos
if it is there, or if the directory it would be made in is there;
otherwise it names substrate's.  So a file a program makes as
.I /tmp/x
is in
.IR /perso/sunos/tmp ,
and is the file it then finds by that name.  The arguments of a program
being started and the text of a symbolic link are passed as given.
.PP
.I /dev
is the kernel's, whatever the tree holds there.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /perso/sunos
The root of a SunOS installation.
.TP
.I /perso/sunos/lib/ld.so
The dynamic linker a program maps for itself.
.TP
.I /perso/sunos/usr/lib/libc.so.2.0
The C library it maps.
.SH NOTES
.I tools/sunos/build\-image.sh
builds an image for
.I /perso/sunos
from the distribution's floppies, which are not part of substrate.
.PP
With that image, the system's Bourne and C shells run scripts and
interactive sessions with job control;
.BR ls ,
.BR cat ,
.BR sed ,
.BR awk ,
.BR sort ,
.BR find ,
.BR ed ,
.BR vi
and
.BR more
work; and its C compiler compiles and links programs, dynamically and
statically, that then run.
.SH BUGS
A system call SunOS 4.0 has and this personality does not fails with
.BR EINVAL ;
SunOS has no
.BR ENOSYS .
These are not provided:
.BR ptrace (2),
.BR profil (2)
beyond substrate's own,
.BR mount (2)
and
.BR unmount (2),
.BR reboot (2),
.BR swapon (2),
.BR settimeofday (2)
and
.BR adjtime (2),
.BR mincore (2),
.BR quotactl (2),
the NFS calls,
.BR getmsg (2)
and
.BR putmsg (2),
the System V IPC calls, and record locking through
.BR fcntl (2).
.BR flock (2)
succeeds without locking.
.PP
.BR ps (1)
and the other programs that read kernel memory through
.I /dev/kmem
and
.I /dev/drum
do not work.
.PP
.BR ttyname (3)
finds no name for a pseudo-terminal, which SunOS expects in
.I /dev
and substrate has in
.IR /dev/pts ,
so
.BR tty (1)
prints "not a tty".
.PP
The SunOS 4.0.2 upgrade is not applied to the image, and programs of
SunOS 4.1 have not been tried.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR svr3 (4),
.BR svr4 (4),
.BR xenix (4)
